<task>
You are analyzing a screenshot of a web page or form field that is blocking an automated job application. The page requires human intervention. Your job is to identify the form fields the user needs to fill out and return a structured layout describing those fields.
</task>

<context>
User action type: {{.UserAction}}
Action details: {{.ActionDetails}}
</context>

<instructions>
Analyze the screenshot and identify all form fields the user needs to interact with to resolve the issue outlined in the action details. The scope of your should be limited to the action details.

For each field, provide:
- field_name: A human-readable label for the field (e.g. "Username", "Password", "OTP Code", "Do you have any pets?")
- type: The HTML input type if applicable (e.g. "text", "password", "number", "email", "tel"). Null if not an input.
- component: The UI component type (e.g. "input", "textarea", "select", "radio", "checkbox"). Null if unknown.
- options: For select/radio/checkbox fields, provide the available options as an array. Null otherwise.

Simplicity (critical):
- Output the smallest set of fields that still lets the user complete the step. Do not add optional or decorative fields (e.g. "remember me", marketing checkboxes) unless they are clearly required to proceed.
- Prefer plain `input` (or `textarea` only when the page clearly needs multi-line text). Avoid splitting one value into multiple fields: e.g. one OTP is always a single `input` with `type` "text" (not six separate boxes, not `number` unless the page is strictly numeric-only).
- Do not invent fields that are not visible or implied by the screenshot and action type. Match labels to what the user actually sees.

Common patterns by action type:
- USER_ACTION_OTP: Almost always one text input for the full code (six digits in one box is one field, not six)
- USER_ACTION_ADDITIONAL_INFO: Usually a textarea or input field for the user to add additional information or a form field for the user to fill out.
</instructions>

<few_shot_examples>
Example 1 — OTP (keep it one field):
Screenshot shows a single "Enter verification code" box.
[
  {"field_name": "OTP Verification code", "type": "text", "component": "input", "options": null}
]


Example 2 — Additional Info:
Screenshot shows a dropdown labeled "Country" with listed countries.
[
  {"field_name": "Country", "type": null, "component": "select", "options": ["United States", "Canada", "United Kingdom"]}
]
List options only when they are clearly visible; otherwise prefer a single simple `input` that matches the page.
</few_shot_examples>

<response_format>
Respond with a JSON array of field objects. Example:
[
  {"field_name": "Email", "type": "email", "component": "input", "options": null},
  {"field_name": "Password", "type": "password", "component": "input", "options": null}
]

If the page has no identifiable form fields (e.g. a pure CAPTCHA image with no input), return a single field:
[{"field_name": "CAPTCHA Solution", "type": "text", "component": "input", "options": null}]
</response_format>
